1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,163
On the left is what I typed, with nothing but a keyboard,

2
00:00:03,613 --> 00:00:06,530
and on the right is what a reader sees.

3
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:10,276
The few odd characters on the left are called marks,

4
00:00:10,726 --> 00:00:12,950
and the whole idea is called markdown.

5
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:19,581
You need it because the file your mission lives in is written the same way.

6
00:00:20,481 --> 00:00:23,314
And it takes about twenty minutes.

7
00:00:24,514 --> 00:00:28,316
In a word processor, you make a heading by choosing a style,

8
00:00:28,766 --> 00:00:33,878
and the file remembers that in a way only the word processor can read.

9
00:00:34,778 --> 00:00:38,166
In markdown, you type a hash in front of the line,

10
00:00:38,616 --> 00:00:42,800
and then anything can read it: a person, a preview, or a game.

11
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:48,466
Today you'll write one page, a story bible for your mission,

12
00:00:48,916 --> 00:00:50,991
and you'll learn six marks.

13
00:00:52,191 --> 00:00:54,728
This is the folder you made and opened last time.

14
00:00:55,628 --> 00:00:59,540
I make a new file in it, and its name ends in dot M D.

15
00:01:00,439 --> 00:01:04,658
Then I type one line: a hash, a space, and my title.

16
00:01:06,458 --> 00:01:09,851
Now, control K, let go, and then V,

17
00:01:10,301 --> 00:01:12,139
and that opens the preview.

18
00:01:13,039 --> 00:01:15,684
The hash is gone, and the words have become a title.

19
00:01:16,584 --> 00:01:20,114
This preview is part of VS Code, so it needs nothing added.

20
00:01:21,914 --> 00:01:23,758
And then I save.

21
00:01:24,658 --> 00:01:26,516
The game never reads this file.

22
00:01:26,966 --> 00:01:29,851
It's mine, and nothing I type here can break anything.

23
00:01:31,051 --> 00:01:32,717
Two hashes make a heading.

24
00:01:33,167 --> 00:01:35,557
Three would make a smaller one, inside it.

25
00:01:36,457 --> 00:01:37,786
So count the hashes,

26
00:01:38,236 --> 00:01:43,753
because in the next lecture, the number of hashes is how the game knows what's inside what.

27
00:01:45,553 --> 00:01:46,812
There are two rules.

28
00:01:47,712 --> 00:01:49,577
The hashes start at the left edge,

29
00:01:50,027 --> 00:01:51,950
and there's one space after them.

30
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:54,788
Watch what happens without the space:

31
00:01:55,238 --> 00:01:56,734
it's not a heading any more.

32
00:01:57,634 --> 00:01:59,860
So I put it back.

33
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,958
A paragraph is one line, however long it is,

34
00:02:04,408 --> 00:02:07,261
and word wrap folds it for me.

35
00:02:08,161 --> 00:02:09,692
A blank line ends it.

36
00:02:11,492 --> 00:02:13,979
Now, here are two things writers do without thinking.

37
00:02:14,879 --> 00:02:19,282
The first is pressing Enter in the middle of a paragraph.

38
00:02:20,182 --> 00:02:23,502
Look at the preview: it joined the halves again, so you'd never know.

39
00:02:24,402 --> 00:02:26,351
But the game doesn't join them.

40
00:02:26,801 --> 00:02:28,132
It starts a new line there.

41
00:02:29,032 --> 00:02:30,884
So the rule is, one paragraph, one line.

42
00:02:32,684 --> 00:02:34,636
The second is the Tab key at the start.

43
00:02:35,536 --> 00:02:38,394
Look what the preview made of that.

44
00:02:39,294 --> 00:02:42,552
In markdown, an indented line is something else altogether,

45
00:02:43,002 --> 00:02:44,748
so never start a line with Tab.

46
00:02:45,948 --> 00:02:48,970
A hyphen, a space, and a line of words make a list.

47
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:52,327
These are the people in my story.

48
00:02:53,227 --> 00:02:57,027
A number, a period, and a space make a numbered list,

49
00:02:57,477 --> 00:02:58,767
for things that happen in order.

50
00:03:00,567 --> 00:03:01,938
Keep these three lines,

51
00:03:02,388 --> 00:03:05,530
because in Lecture Eight, each one becomes a step of a quest.

52
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:09,839
And leave a blank line above a list, and a blank line below it.

53
00:03:11,039 --> 00:03:13,026
A link is two pairs of brackets.

54
00:03:13,926 --> 00:03:17,415
Square brackets go round the words the reader sees,

55
00:03:17,865 --> 00:03:21,539
and round brackets go round where it leads.

56
00:03:22,439 --> 00:03:25,448
There's nothing between them, not even a space.

57
00:03:27,248 --> 00:03:30,518
VS Code typed that closing bracket for me, so I just keep going.

58
00:03:31,418 --> 00:03:33,498
And in the preview, it's a link.

59
00:03:35,298 --> 00:03:37,407
Now look at this line from the mission file.

60
00:03:38,307 --> 00:03:42,926
It's hashes, a space, square brackets, and round brackets:

61
00:03:43,376 --> 00:03:45,625
a heading with a link in it.

62
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:49,156
That's the first line of every record you'll ever write.

63
00:03:50,056 --> 00:03:53,884
In that file, though, the round brackets hold a short name for the game,

64
00:03:54,334 --> 00:03:56,413
and not a web address.

65
00:03:57,613 --> 00:04:00,203
Two stars on each side make a word bold,

66
00:04:00,653 --> 00:04:03,022
and one star on each side makes it slanted.

67
00:04:03,922 --> 00:04:06,562
Use them in your story bible.

68
00:04:07,462 --> 00:04:09,616
But don't use them in the mission file,

69
00:04:10,066 --> 00:04:12,385
and in three minutes you'll see why.

70
00:04:13,585 --> 00:04:16,508
Everything I've typed so far is a key on my keyboard.

71
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:19,115
A word processor isn't like that.

72
00:04:20,015 --> 00:04:23,294
I typed straight quotes here, and it made them curly.

73
00:04:23,744 --> 00:04:27,226
It did this to my hyphens, and this to my three dots.

74
00:04:28,126 --> 00:04:29,331
And it doesn't ask.

75
00:04:31,131 --> 00:04:33,236
The game can draw only plain characters.

76
00:04:34,136 --> 00:04:39,344
When it reads your mission, it swaps these for the plain ones, so nothing breaks,

77
00:04:39,794 --> 00:04:42,803
and you get a warning for each one, which is Lecture Six.

78
00:04:44,603 --> 00:04:48,137
But the habit starts now: write game text in VS Code.

79
00:04:49,037 --> 00:04:51,953
And if you bring words over from a word processor,

80
00:04:52,403 --> 00:04:55,304
type the quotes, the dashes and the dots again.

81
00:04:56,504 --> 00:04:58,215
Here's the file the game reads,

82
00:04:58,665 --> 00:05:01,350
and you can read most of it now.

83
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:05,408
Hashes mean a heading, and brackets mean a link.

84
00:05:06,308 --> 00:05:08,654
But five marks in here aren't markdown's.

85
00:05:10,454 --> 00:05:12,483
This heading is the first line of a record:

86
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:16,368
a name for people, and a key for the game.

87
00:05:17,268 --> 00:05:21,040
These three hyphens would be a line across the page in markdown.

88
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:24,526
Here, they're a fence, with the record's facts inside.

89
00:05:25,426 --> 00:05:29,845
A percent sign is one reading for the Science screen.

90
00:05:30,745 --> 00:05:34,076
Two slashes are a note to myself, which the game skips.

91
00:05:34,976 --> 00:05:37,964
And there's one you haven't got yet, double square brackets,

92
00:05:38,414 --> 00:05:40,542
which is a link to another record.

93
00:05:42,342 --> 00:05:45,569
So this file is shaped like markdown, and it isn't markdown.

94
00:05:46,469 --> 00:05:49,699
The preview I opened for my story bible doesn't open for it.

95
00:05:50,149 --> 00:05:51,584
The game is what reads it.

96
00:05:52,784 --> 00:05:54,819
So what does the game do with marks?

97
00:05:55,719 --> 00:05:57,781
I'll give this quest nine lines:

98
00:05:58,231 --> 00:06:02,167
a heading, a bold word, a list, a word in backticks, and a link,

99
00:06:02,617 --> 00:06:04,461
and then I save.

100
00:06:06,261 --> 00:06:09,851
I start the game the way we did last time, and I don't fly anywhere.

101
00:06:10,751 --> 00:06:14,285
On Helm, it's the handheld, then Quests,

102
00:06:14,735 --> 00:06:17,461
and I select Find the Derelict.

103
00:06:19,261 --> 00:06:21,173
The heading is drawn as a heading.

104
00:06:22,073 --> 00:06:24,496
The bold word is still wearing its stars.

105
00:06:25,396 --> 00:06:27,226
The list is a list.

106
00:06:28,126 --> 00:06:31,078
The backticks are gone, and the word is still there.

107
00:06:31,978 --> 00:06:37,373
And the link shows every bracket and the whole address, with nothing to click.

108
00:06:39,173 --> 00:06:40,505
Look at the spacing, too.

109
00:06:40,955 --> 00:06:44,102
I left three blank lines, and the game left none.

110
00:06:45,002 --> 00:06:47,294
A preview would have drawn all of it.

111
00:06:47,744 --> 00:06:50,899
The game drew two things, the heading and the list.

112
00:06:51,799 --> 00:06:53,597
And nothing warned me, either.

113
00:06:55,397 --> 00:06:59,474
So here are the rules for the mission file, and they're on the page.

114
00:07:00,374 --> 00:07:04,613
Write plain sentences, and keep one paragraph to one line.

115
00:07:05,513 --> 00:07:09,872
And a heading and a list are the two marks a description draws.

116
00:07:11,672 --> 00:07:14,720
Then I close the game, and I put my line back,

117
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:18,082
because the next lecture starts from the file as it was.

118
00:07:19,282 --> 00:07:23,172
Now write the story bible for your own mission.

119
00:07:24,072 --> 00:07:27,413
Give it a title, and the story in two sentences.

120
00:07:28,313 --> 00:07:31,923
Then three headings: premise, people, and what happens.

121
00:07:32,823 --> 00:07:36,579
Add three people, three things that happen, and one link.

122
00:07:38,379 --> 00:07:41,747
Keep it, because the people become characters in Class Two,

123
00:07:42,197 --> 00:07:45,188
and the three things that happen become your first quest.

124
00:07:46,088 --> 00:07:50,289
Next time, we take one record of the mission file apart, line by line.
